\label{ch:self-optimizing}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:self-opt-intro}

The traditional boundary between interpreters and compilers is dissolving. Modern language implementations increasingly employ \emph{self-optimizing interpreters}: interpreters that start by executing programs directly but progressively specialize and optimize themselves based on observed runtime behavior. The pinnacle of this approach is the Truffle framework, which uses partial evaluation to automatically transform an interpreter into a JIT compiler.

This chapter explores the spectrum from pure interpretation to full compilation, examining how interpreters can be augmented with profiling, specialization, and self-modification to achieve performance that approaches or exceeds traditional JIT compilation---with a fraction of the implementation effort.

\section{AST Interpreter Specialization}\label{sec:ast-specialization}

\subsection{The AST Interpreter}\label{sec:ast-interpreter}

An AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) interpreter directly executes the program's parse tree. Each node in the tree has an \texttt{execute} method that performs the node's operation.

\begin{definition}[AST Interpreter]\label{def:ast-interpreter}
An \emph{AST interpreter} is an interpreter that executes a program by traversing its abstract syntax tree, calling an \texttt{execute} method on each node.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Simple AST Nodes]\label{ex:ast-nodes}
\begin{verbatim}
  abstract class ExprNode {
      abstract Object execute(Frame frame);
  }

  class AddNode extends ExprNode {
      ExprNode left, right;
      Object execute(Frame frame) {
          Object l = left.execute(frame);
          Object r = right.execute(frame);
          if (l instanceof Integer && r instanceof Integer)
              return (Integer)l + (Integer)r;
          // ... handle other types
      }
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{The Specialization Problem}\label{sec:specialization-problem}

The naive AST interpreter checks types at every operation. For a program that always adds integers, this check is wasted work on every execution. \emph{Specialization} replaces the generic node with a type-specific version after observing the actual types.

\begin{definition}[Node Specialization]\label{def:node-specialization}
\emph{Node specialization} is the process of replacing a generic AST node with a specialized version that handles a specific case more efficiently, based on observed runtime behavior.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Self-Modifying ASTs}\label{sec:self-modifying-ast}

In a self-modifying AST, nodes rewrite themselves:

\begin{example}[Specializing Add Node]\label{ex:specializing-add}
\begin{verbatim}
  class GenericAddNode extends ExprNode {
      ExprNode left, right;
      int executionCount = 0;

      Object execute(Frame frame) {
          Object l = left.execute(frame);
          Object r = right.execute(frame);
          if (l instanceof Integer && r instanceof Integer) {
              executionCount++;
              if (executionCount > THRESHOLD) {
                  // Specialize: replace self with integer add node
                  parent.replaceChild(this,
                      new IntAddNode(left, right));
              }
              return (Integer)l + (Integer)r;
          }
          // ... handle other types
      }
  }

  class IntAddNode extends ExprNode {
      ExprNode left, right;
      Object execute(Frame frame) {
          // No type checks needed!
          return (Integer)left.execute(frame)
               + (Integer)right.execute(frame);
      }
  }
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Polymorphic Inline Caching}\label{sec:polymorphic-ic}

Nodes can maintain an inline cache of observed types, forming a chain of specialized nodes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Uninitialized}: no types observed yet. The node executes generically and profiles the types.
  \item \textbf{Monomorphic}: one type observed. A specialized node handles that type directly.
  \item \textbf{Polymorphic}: a few types observed. A chain of type-specific nodes, each checking its type and falling through to the next.
  \item \textbf{Megamorphic}: many types observed. Falls back to the generic implementation.
\end{itemize}

\section{Quickening}\label{sec:quickening}

\subsection{The Quickening Concept}\label{sec:quickening-concept}

\emph{Quickening} is a technique from the Python world where bytecode instructions are replaced with specialized versions at runtime.

\begin{definition}[Quickening]\label{def:quickening}
\emph{Quickening} is the runtime replacement of generic bytecode instructions with specialized versions that skip type checks and dispatch for the observed types.
\end{definition}

\subsection{CPython's Quickening}\label{sec:cpython-quickening}

CPython 3.11+ implements quickening:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Bytecode is first ``quickened'' from generic instructions (e.g., \texttt{BINARY\_OP}) to type-specific instructions (e.g., \texttt{BINARY\_OP\_INT\_ADD}).
  \item The quickened instructions skip the type-dispatch overhead.
  \item If the types change, the instruction is dequickened back to the generic version.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Quickening in Other Systems}\label{sec:quickening-other}

Quickening-like techniques appear in many systems:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Ruby (YJIR)}: YARV bytecode is specialized based on observed types.
  \item \textbf{PHP (JIT)}: Zend VM opcodes are specialized for observed operand types.
  \item \textbf{Lua}: LuaJIT traces are specialized for the types observed during tracing.
\end{itemize}

\section{Truffle Framework}\label{sec:truffle}

\subsection{Overview}\label{sec:truffle-overview}

Truffle is a language implementation framework built on the JVM (or GraalVM) that uses \emph{partial evaluation} to automatically transform an interpreter into a JIT compiler.

\begin{definition}[Truffle Framework]\label{def:truffle}
\emph{Truffle} is a language implementation framework that provides:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A node-based AST interpreter framework with self-specializing nodes.
  \item Automatic partial evaluation of the interpreter to produce optimized machine code.
  \item Infrastructure for profiling, inline caching, and on-stack replacement.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{How Truffle Works}\label{sec:truffle-how}

The key insight of Truffle is the \emph{first Futamura projection}: partial evaluation of an interpreter with respect to a program yields compiled code for that program.

\begin{enumerate}
  \item The language implementer writes an AST interpreter using Truffle's node framework.
  \item Nodes self-specialize based on observed types (as described above).
  \item After sufficient specialization, the Graal compiler partially evaluates the interpreter with respect to the program being executed.
  \item The result is machine code equivalent to what a hand-written JIT compiler would produce.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{insight}
Truffle's brilliance is that it eliminates the need to write a separate JIT compiler for each language. The language implementer writes only the interpreter, annotating nodes with specialization logic. The partial evaluator does the rest, producing optimized code automatically. This dramatically reduces the effort required to implement a high-performance language.
\end{insight}

\subsection{Truffle Nodes and Frames}\label{sec:truffle-nodes}

Truffle provides:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Node classes}: base classes for AST nodes with specialization support.
  \item \textbf{Frame descriptors}: describe the local variables of a function, enabling the partial evaluator to allocate them efficiently.
  \item \textbf{Call targets}: abstract function calls that support inline caching and inlining.
  \item \textbf{Assumptions}: invalidate compiled code when global conditions change (e.g., a new method is defined).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Truffle Languages}\label{sec:truffle-languages}

Languages implemented with Truffle include:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Graal.js}: JavaScript
  \item \textbf{GraalPython}: Python
  \item \textbf{TruffleRuby}: Ruby
  \item \textbf{Sulong}: C/C++ (via LLVM bitcode)
  \item \textbf{SimpleLanguage}: a tutorial language demonstrating Truffle's features
\end{itemize}

\section{Partial Evaluation for Interpreters}\label{sec:partial-eval}

\subsection{Partial Evaluation}\label{sec:pe-concept}

\begin{definition}[Partial Evaluation]\label{def:partial-eval}
\emph{Partial evaluation} is a program transformation that takes a program $P$ and some of its inputs (the \emph{static} inputs) and produces a \emph{residual program} $P'$ that is specialized to those static inputs. Running $P'$ with the remaining (dynamic) inputs produces the same result as running $P$ with all inputs.
\[
  P' = \text{PE}(P, \text{static\_input})
\]
\[
  P'(\text{dynamic\_input}) = P(\text{static\_input}, \text{dynamic\_input})
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{The First Futamura Projection}\label{sec:futamura-1}

\begin{theorem}[First Futamura Projection]\label{thm:futamura-1}
Partial evaluation of an interpreter with respect to a source program yields compiled code for that program:
\[
  \text{PE}(\text{interp}, \text{source}) = \text{compiled\_code}
\]
where $\text{compiled\_code}(\text{input}) = \text{interp}(\text{source}, \text{input})$.
\end{theorem}

This theorem, due to Yoshihiko Futamura (1971), is the theoretical foundation of Truffle's approach.

\subsection{Binding-Time Analysis}\label{sec:bta}

For partial evaluation to work, the system must determine which computations can be performed at partial evaluation time (static) and which must be deferred to runtime (dynamic).

\begin{definition}[Binding-Time Analysis]\label{def:bta}
A \emph{binding-time analysis} (BTA) classifies each expression in the interpreter as either:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Static}: can be computed during partial evaluation (depends only on the source program).
  \item \textbf{Dynamic}: must be computed at runtime (depends on the input).
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

In Truffle, the BTA is performed by the Graal compiler's partial evaluator. The interpreter's structure (node types, specialization decisions) is static; the data values are dynamic.

\subsection{Challenges in Partial Evaluation}\label{sec:pe-challenges}

Partial evaluation of interpreters faces several challenges:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Metacircularity}: the interpreter may use features that the partial evaluator cannot handle (reflection, dynamic code generation).
  \item \textbf{Polyvariant specialization}: the same interpreter code may need to be specialized differently for different contexts.
  \item \textbf{Termination}: the partial evaluator must terminate, even if the interpreter does not (for non-terminating programs).
  \item \textbf{Code explosion}: aggressive specialization can produce very large residual programs.
\end{itemize}

\section{Feedback-Driven Optimization}\label{sec:feedback-driven}

\subsection{Profiling Infrastructure}\label{sec:profiling}

Self-optimizing interpreters collect profiling information to guide specialization:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Type profiles}: what types are observed at each operation?
  \item \textbf{Value profiles}: what values are observed? (e.g., for switch dispatch)
  \item \textbf{Branch profiles}: which branches are taken more often?
  \item \textbf{Call profiles}: what targets are observed at each call site?
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Adaptive Optimization}\label{sec:adaptive}

The interpreter adapts its behavior based on profiles:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Initially, all nodes execute generically and collect profiles.
  \item After observing a stable pattern, nodes specialize.
  \item If the pattern changes (e.g., a new type is observed), nodes may de-specialize or add new specializations.
  \item If the pattern is too complex (megamorphic), nodes fall back to generic execution.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Phase Behavior}\label{sec:phase-behavior}

Programs often exhibit \emph{phase behavior}: different types are used in different phases of execution. Self-optimizing interpreters must handle this:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Re-specialization}: when a new phase begins, nodes re-specialize for the new types.
  \item \textbf{Polymorphic caches}: maintain multiple specializations simultaneously.
  \item \textbf{Aging}: gradually forget old profiles to adapt to new phases.
\end{itemize}

\section{From Interpreter to Compiler: The Spectrum}\label{sec:spectrum}

\subsection{The Interpretation--Compilation Spectrum}\label{sec:interp-comp-spectrum}

