The boundary between interpretation and compilation is not sharp. Each step along the spectrum adds more optimization and complexity:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Pure interpreter}: no optimization, no profiling. Simple but slow.
  \item \textbf{Quickened interpreter}: bytecode specialization, basic profiling. Moderate improvement.
  \item \textbf{Self-optimizing interpreter}: AST node specialization, inline caches. Significant improvement.
  \item \textbf{Trace-based JIT}: records execution traces, compiles them to machine code. Major improvement.
  \item \textbf{Method-based JIT}: compiles entire methods with full optimization. Best steady-state performance.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Truffle's Position on the Spectrum}\label{sec:truffle-position}

Truffle occupies a unique position: it starts as a self-optimizing interpreter and, through partial evaluation, becomes a method-based JIT compiler. The language implementer writes only the interpreter; the partial evaluator handles the compilation. This collapses the entire spectrum into a single implementation effort.

\section{Performance of Self-Optimizing Interpreters}\label{sec:self-opt-perf}

\subsection{Benchmark Results}\label{sec:benchmark-results}

Truffle-based language implementations have achieved impressive performance:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Graal.js}: competitive with or faster than V8 on many JavaScript benchmarks.
  \item \textbf{TruffleRuby}: comparable to MRI Ruby with JIT, approaching JRuby performance.
  \item \textbf{GraalPython}: significantly faster than CPython for compute-intensive code.
  \item \textbf{SimpleLanguage}: a tutorial language that demonstrates Truffle's capabilities, achieving near-native performance after specialization.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Warm-Up Behavior}\label{sec:warmup}

Self-optimizing interpreters exhibit characteristic warm-up behavior:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Cold start}: the interpreter runs at interpretation speed.
  \item \textbf{Specialization}: after a few iterations, nodes begin to specialize.
  \item \textbf{Partial evaluation}: the Graal compiler compiles the specialized interpreter.
  \item \textbf{Peak performance}: the compiled code runs at near-native speed.
\end{enumerate}

The warm-up time depends on the program and the number of specializations needed. For programs with stable types, warm-up is fast. For programs with many type transitions, warm-up is slower.

