\section{Implementing a Self-Optimizing Interpreter}\label{sec:implementing}

\subsection{Step-by-Step Guide}\label{sec:step-by-step}

Implementing a self-optimizing interpreter with Truffle involves:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Define AST node classes for each language construct.
  \item Implement \texttt{execute} methods with type checks and specialization logic.
  \item Use Truffle's \texttt{@Specialization} annotations to declare type-specific implementations.
  \item Implement call targets with inline caching.
  \item Define frame descriptors for local variables.
  \item Run on the GraalVM with the Truffle framework.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Common Pitfalls}\label{sec:pitfalls}

Common mistakes when implementing self-optimizing interpreters:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Insufficient specialization}: nodes that do not specialize miss optimization opportunities.
  \item \textbf{Over-specialization}: too many specializations increase code size and compilation time.
  \item \textbf{Missing invalidation}: failing to invalidate compiled code when global state changes.
  \item \textbf{Incorrect frame access}: accessing frame slots incorrectly after partial evaluation.
  \item \textbf{Allocation in hot paths}: allocating objects in frequently-executed code defeats the GC.
\end{itemize}

