\section{Quickening in Depth}\label{sec:quickening-depth}

Quickening is a lightweight optimization technique that provides significant speedup with minimal implementation effort. This section examines its mechanics across different systems.

\subsection{CPython's Adaptive Interpreter}\label{sec:cpython-adaptive}

CPython 3.11+ implements an \emph{adaptive specializing interpreter} that quickens bytecode at runtime:

\begin{verbatim}
  // Original bytecode:
  BINARY_OP      0 (ADD)    // Generic: checks types at runtime
  
  // After quickening (int + int observed):
  BINARY_OP_INT_ADD          // Specialized: no type check
  
  // After quickening (str + str observed):
  BINARY_OP_STR_ADD          // Specialized: no type check
  
  // If types change (float observed after int specialization):
  BINARY_OP          0 (ADD) // Dequickened: back to generic
\end{verbatim}

The quickening process:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Each instruction starts in its generic form.
  \item After a counter reaches a threshold (typically 1 execution), the instruction is ``adapted'' to the observed types.
  \item The adapted instruction is a specialized opcode that skips the type check.
  \item A version counter tracks whether the specialization is still valid.
  \item If the types change, the instruction is dequickened.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Quickening vs.\ Truffle Specialization}\label{sec:quickening-vs-truffle}

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Quickening vs.\ Truffle node specialization}
\label{tab:quickening-vs-truffle}
\begin{tabular}{p{3.5cm}p{5cm}p{5cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Aspect} & \textbf{Quickening} & \textbf{Truffle Specialization} \\
\hline
Representation & Bytecode replacement & AST node replacement \\
Granularity & Single instruction & Entire subexpression \\
Type tracking & Per-instruction & Per-node with guards \\
Compilation & None (interpreted) & Partial evaluation to machine code \\
Deoptimization & Dequickening & Assumption invalidation \\
Peak performance & 1.5--3$\times$ speedup & 10--100$\times$ speedup \\
Implementation effort & Low & Medium--High \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Quickening for Other Operations}\label{sec:quickening-operations}

Quickening can be applied to many operations beyond arithmetic:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Attribute access}: specialize for the observed object shape (e.g., \texttt{LOAD\_ATTR\_DICT}, \texttt{LOAD\_ATTR\_SLOT}).
  \item \textbf{Function calls}: specialize for the observed callee (monomorphic inline caching).
  \item \textbf{Subscript access}: specialize for the observed container type (list, dict, tuple).
  \item \textbf{Comparison}: specialize for the observed operand types.
  \item \textbf{Iteration}: specialize for the observed iterator type (list iterator, dict iterator, range).
\end{itemize}

