\section{Feedback-Driven Optimization in Depth}\label{sec:feedback-depth}

Self-optimizing interpreters rely on runtime profiling to guide optimization decisions. This section examines the profiling infrastructure and the optimization strategies it enables.

\subsection{Profiling Data Structures}\label{sec:profile-structures}

Different kinds of profiling data require different data structures:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Profiling data structures}
\label{tab:profile-structures}
\begin{tabular}{lp{4cm}p{4cm}}
\hline
\textbf{Profile Type} & \textbf{Data Structure} & \textbf{Example} \\
\hline
Type profile & Inline cache (1--4 entries) & \texttt{[Integer, Double, \_, \_]} \\
Value profile & Value counter + top-$k$ & \texttt{count=1000, top=[0, 1, -1]} \\
Branch profile & Branch counter & \texttt{taken=800, not\_taken=200} \\
Call profile & Polymorphic inline cache & \texttt{[func\_A, func\_B, \_, \_]} \\
Shape profile & Shape/map transitions & \texttt{shape=\{x:0, y:1\}} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Feedback-Driven Inlining}\label{sec:feedback-inlining}

Self-optimizing interpreters use call profiles to decide which functions to inline:

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Feedback-Driven Inlining Decision}\label{alg:feedback-inline}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Function{ShouldInline}{$\mathrm{call\_site}$}
  \State $\mathrm{profile} \gets \mathrm{call\_site.profile}$
  \If{$\mathrm{profile}$ is monomorphic}
    \State $\mathrm{callee} \gets \mathrm{profile.targets}[0]$
    \If{$\mathrm{callee.size} < \mathrm{INLINE\_THRESHOLD}$}
      \State \Return \textbf{true} \Comment{Inline the single target}
    \EndIf
  \ElsIf{$\mathrm{profile}$ is polymorphic ($\leq 3$ targets)}
    \State \Return \textbf{true} \Comment{Inline all targets with type guards}
  \EndIf
  \State \Return \textbf{false} \Comment{Megamorphic: too many targets}
\EndFunction
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Speculative Optimization and Deoptimization}\label{sec:speculative-deopt}

Self-optimizing interpreters make speculative assumptions based on profiles. When an assumption is violated, the system must \emph{deoptimize}:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Detect}: a type check or guard fails, indicating that the speculation is no longer valid.
  \item \textbf{Materialize}: reconstruct the interpreter state (local variables, operand stack) from the compiled state.
  \item \textbf{Transfer}: transfer control to the interpreter at the deoptimization point.
  \item \textbf{Re-specialize}: the interpreter re-profiles and re-specializes for the new behavior.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{verbatim}
  // Speculative optimization example:
  // Profile: x is always Integer
  // Compiled code:
  //   mov (%rax), %rbx         ; load x (assumed Integer)
  //   add %rcx, %rbx           ; add directly (no type check)
  //   jo deopt                 ; overflow -> deoptimize
  //   test $INT_TAG, %rbx      ; verify tag (can be removed)
  //
  // If x becomes a Float:
  //   Guard fails -> deoptimize
  //   Materialize interpreter state
  //   Resume interpretation
  //   Re-profile: x is Float -> specialize for Float
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Phase Change Detection}\label{sec:phase-change}

Programs often exhibit \emph{phase behavior}: different types or call targets dominate at different points in execution. Self-optimizing interpreters must detect and adapt to phase changes:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Aging counters}: profile counters are gradually decremented, so old profiles lose influence over time.
  \item \textbf{Epoch-based profiles}: profiles are reset at epoch boundaries (e.g., every $N$ iterations of a loop).
  \item \textbf{Multiple specialization}: the interpreter maintains multiple specialized versions simultaneously, switching between them based on the current type.
\end{itemize}

